      "id": 1,
      "text": "Traditional Yup'ik lineages in Tununak divided labor—men hunted, women processed hides and foods—while elders used storytelling to keep fishing sites in oral maps.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/tununak_alaska",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Russian missionaries first reached Tununak in the 18th‑19th centuries, bringing Orthodox Christianity and trade that gradually reshaped local spiritual practices.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/tununak_alaska",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Long before European arrival, the Qaluyaarmiut Yup'ik people called the area Tununeq and left 47 archaeological sites across Nelson Island.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/tununak_alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Tununak sits on Nelson Island’s northwest coast, overlooking the Bering Sea’s rugged shoreline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tununak%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Edward Nelson first documented Tununak in December 1878, noting only six residents—five Yup'ik and one trader.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tununak%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The community’s name changed from Tanunak in 1940 to the current Tununak when it incorporated in 1975.",
